From STEVEN.P.MILLARD at prodigy.net  Thu Sep 16 16:46:50 1999
From: STEVEN.P.MILLARD at prodigy.net (STEVEN.P.MILLARD)
Date: Thu, 16 Sep 1999 09:46:50 -0700
Subject: [S] References for late discovery of hole in the ozone layer due to "outlier" deletion
Message-ID: <01BF002F.88042860.STEVEN.P.MILLARD@prodigy.net>

Hello Everyone,

Thanks so much for your quick replies to my query about a reference 
citing the delayed discovery of the hole in the ozone layer over 
Antarctica due to software that automatically deleted "outliers".  Anne 
York's response reminded me that NOVA had a program on the ozone hole 
quite a few years ago, and now I'm thinking that is where I first heard 
about the "outlier" deletion episode.

Thanks to Gene D. Felber for pointing out the URL 
http://curriculum.qed.qld.gov.au/kla/eda/ozone.htm which gives a brief 
overview of this exact topic!!

Also thanks to Keith Dixon, who wrote:

I believe that the reference is...
Stolarski, R.S., A.J. Krueger, M.R. Schoeberl, R.D. McPeters, P.A. 
Newman, and J.C. Alpert. 1986. Nimbus 7 satellite measurements of the 
springtime Antarctic ozone decrease. Nature 322:808-811.
On his web page on "DATA QUALITY CONTROL / QUALITY ASSURANCE" Don 
Edwards of the Department of Statistics, University of South Carolina 
describes it as "One of the most famous data QA/QC blunders occurred 
when NASA computers were programmed to delete satellite observations of 
ozone concentrations below a specified level, and thus failed to 
discover the "ozone hole" over the south pole (Stolarski et al. 1986)."
  http://www.lternet.edu/ecoinformatics/guide/edwards.fv4.htm
NASA's fact sheets usually omit any direct reference to the outlier 
problem.  On one hand, they'll say "Since the launch of the first TOMS 
aboard the Nimbus-7 polar-orbiting satellite in 1978, NASA has provided 
scientists with reliable, high-resolution daily maps of global ozone 
levels."  Yet they also note, "The ozone hole was discovered through 
British ground-based observations in the mid-1980s, but analysis of 
TOMS data indicates it has existed since at least 1979." It is left for 
the reader to deduce why the "reliable, high-resolution daily maps" 
that NASA was getting since 1978 didn't discover the ozone hole 
earlier.
  http://pao.gsfc.nasa.gov/gsfc/service/gallery/fact_sheets/earthsci  
/ozonestu.htm
Hope that helps,
