From bhm at math.uio.no  Fri Sep 17 14:43:42 1999
From: bhm at math.uio.no (=?utf-8?q?Bj=C3=B8rn-Helge_Mevik?=)
Date: Fri, 17 Sep 1999 16:43:42 +0200
Subject: [S] Summary: Referencing Splus in paper?
References: <s3shflux5wc.fsf@janus.uio.no>
Message-ID: <s3sd7vhwqch.fsf@janus.uio.no>

I apologize for waiting so long with the summary.  Thanks to Pierre
Duchesne <duchesne at DMS.UMontreal.CA>, Stefan Hrafn Jonsson
<stefanj at pop.psu.edu> and SmithSJ at mar.dfo-mpo.gc.ca for enlightening
answers.

Here is the original posting:

[Bjørn-Helge Mevik]

> I'm using Splus for calculations and plots in a statistical paper.  Is
> there a canonical reference to use in the bibliography?  (Or is it not
> common to acknowledge computer programs in journal papers?)

SmithSJ at mar.dfo-mpo.gc.ca suggested using

 @MANUAL{SP2000,
   title =        "S-PLUS 2000 Guide to Statistics, Volume I",
   author = "Mathsoft",
   organization = "Data Analysis Products Division, MathSoft",
   address =      "Seattle, WA",
   year =         "1999"
}

and Pierre Duchesne mentioned

 @MANUAL{Splus,
       TITLE="S-PLUS Reference Manual",
       AUTHOR="Statistical Sciences, {Inc.}",
       NOTE="Seattle: Statistical Science, Inc.",
       YEAR=1991}

As to whether to refer or not, Pierre Duchesne wrote

> My feeling is that if your software/functions are well written and
> could be used by other people without extra effort, it is a good idea
> to cite that your work has been done with Splus.  If you did not wrote
> new useful functions (maybe using Splus as a calculator or something
> like that), it is maybe not a good idea.

Stefan Hrafn Jonsson suggested to look at previous publications in the
journal of interest "to see if software/programming language should be
in the bibliography".

-- 
Bjørn-Helge Mevik <bhm at math.uio.no>
