Message-ID: <01BF00FA.71DB88A0.STEVEN.P.MILLARD@prodigy.net>

Hello Everyone,

Scott Chasalow actually read in depth some of the material posted on 
one of the web sites regarding the late discovery of the ozone hole 
above Antarctica due to "discarding outliers".  According to this 
source (see information below my signature), observations less than 180 
DU were not automatically deleted but instead were "flagged".  So 
instead of never having a chance to see the low values, it was then up 
to the people who dealt with these data to decide whether to include 
these flagged observations in their analyses or not.  I'm not sure 
whether this makes the people who handled the Nimbus-7 data look better 
or worse (of course I always do the right thing with my statistical 
analyses and have never make [sic] a mistake), but the point is that 
someone, somewhere decided to ignore these "outliers."

Sincerely,

