Many, many thanks to all who responded.  It seems that there is
substantial interest in this general topic, although no-one knew
of the particular item (comparing Hemingway with [???] Henry James).
(I'm ***sure*** I didn't imagine this, but never mind.)

Below is a digest of all the responses I received.  Most of them
were posted to the list, so you will have seen them already, but
I thought it might be useful to put them together in a compact
form.

Again thanks to all who responded.

                                        cheers,

                                                Rolf Turner
                                                rolf at math.unb.ca

===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===
(1) From: Christian Leger <leger at DMS.UMontreal.CA>

Not related to Hemingway, but here are two on Shakespeare:
    
Efron, Bradley;Thisted, Ronald
Estimating the number of unseen species: How many words did Shakespeare know?
76Biomtrka 63     435- 448 J
Empirical Bayes;Euler transformation;Negative binomial

Thisted, Ronald;Efron, Bradley
Did Shakespeare write a newly-discovered poem?
87Biomtrka 74     445- 455 J
Empirical Bayes estimation;Poisson regression

===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===
(2) From: Anne York <york at orca.akctr.noaa.gov>

There was an example of this sort discussing authorship of the
Federalist Papers  in: 

Mosteller and Tukey, 1977. Data analysis and regression: A
secondary course in statistics. Addison-Wesley.580p. 

===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===
(3) From: Mario Cortina Borja <cortina at stats.ox.ac.uk>

There's a few papers on the subject of Stylometrics. 
Some people have compared word lengths, other words within 
contexts, others word frequencies.  

I don't know of a comparison between Hemingway and Henry James, but
some good starting points may be:

Thisted & Efron (1987) Biometrika, p. 445-455 (they try  to decide if
Shakespeare did write a newly discovered sonnet)

Holmes (1992) JRSS series A, (Holmes ananlyses several texts written
by Joseph Smith, the founder of the Mormon church in two ways: first
as himself, and secondly when he was allegedly being dictated the
words by an angel)

Holmes (1994) Computers and the Humanities (this is a good article on
authorship)

Mosteller and Wallace (1989) in Tanur (ed.) Statistics: a guide to
the unknown (This is the famous analysis of autorship for the
Federalist papers)

More recently, Binongo and Smith (1999) J. of Applied Statistics,
vol. 26-7, p. 781 provide a study of Oscar Wilde's works and Tweedie
et al. (Computers and the Humanities, 1996 and 1998) have re-analysed
the  Federalist papers.

There's also something about Plato and on St Paul's writings in the
book by Cox & Snell 'Applied Statistics', but I don't have it near me
at the moment.

As it happens, I've just supervised an MSc student here in Oxford
(Constantinos Chappas) on the subject.  We used about 264 texts from
the British Natinoal Corpus.  They came from several kinds of
newspapers and other periodicals as well as news scripts (from TV).
It's striking to see how different the patterns in style are:  there
are some very clearly defined clusters, with tabloids and TV scripts
(news only) having the poorest vocabulary distributions, with the
national broadsheets (Independent, Guardian and Daily Telegraph)
performing better than the 'other' national broadsheets (Belfast
Telegraph and the Scotsman).  We're at the moment writing a paper
about this.

===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===+===
