(13) From: gordon.harrington at uni.edu

Hopefully someone else will be able to point you to specific
literature. If not it may be helpful to know that analysis of word
counts, word length, etc., became big in the mid to late 50's when
Harvard had just added "Psycholinguistics" to its basket of Ph.D.
programs. I had my students do such an analysis of "Robinson Crusoe"
in the late 50's. They compared with data from diaries of normal
lives and of long term isolates and found differences which were also
reflected in different time periods in the novel. If I remember
correctly some of such linguistic data were referenced in David
McClelland's "Personality".

