(14) From: Tom Burr <tburr at nis.lanl.gov>

Can't help you just off the top of my head just where, but I've
definitely seen studies of multiple authors, each with multiple
works, with each work represented by "n-grams" (frequency counts of
each 2-letter word, 3 letter word, ... n-letter word, where "word"
might be loosely defined). That setup is ready for any of many
possible pattern recognition methods and if memory serves, virtually
any "reasonable" n-gram representation will lead to good author
"signatures" (good pattern recognition performance) when you would
expect it (ie, Shakespeare uses different words and word lengths than
Hemingway for example), so your:

   > The surprizing conclusion of the study was that the two
   > authors could ***not*** be distinguished on this basis.

is a surprise if we compare Shakespeare to Hemingway, but
maybe not if we compare ?? to Hemingway.

I'll try to find a reference, but the "text retrieval" community
is a good start:

http://www.soi.city.ac.uk/research/cisr/trec/trec.html

They consider much "harder" problems than "retrive all documents
that "look like" (in n-gram sense) query document (could easily set
up to see if 2 or more authors have distinct n-gram signatures)

